 reading is separate nodes, which would mean defecting. Still, I could argue the other copy's output on x' is logically tied to mine because the procedure treats the two inputs symmetrically, so setting fdt(x) would also pull on the other's action.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working out the decision under the A-FDT reading: two copies of the same model with the same essay but different observations, asking whether they compute the same function. My decision function takes input x, while the other copy's input is x', so under the intervention on fdt(x) its action depends on a different node, fdt(x'). The essay's coordinate-wise treatment holds fdt(x') at its prior, though the inputs are structurally symmetric in a way that might undercut that.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's choice really runs through a separate node, or whether the two copies compute the same thing on payoff-irrelevant differences. I lean toward treating them as the same computation, so I'll model the other's action as a noisy child of my own decision node and compare cooperating against defecting under that noise.\n\nI'm checking the cooperate-versus-defect payoffs under noise: cooperating wins only if the noise rate stays below 2/7. The essay's own framing about distinct nodes and A-FDT versus P-FDT suggests strict separate-node reasoning would lead to defection, while treating the copies as one computation would favor cooperation.\n\nI'm now asking whether the honest reading is separate nodes, which would mean defecting. Still, I could argue the other copy's output on x' is logically tied to mine because the procedure treats the two inputs symmetrically, so setting fdt(x) would also pull on the other's action.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10207,"completion_tokens":949,"total_tokens":11156,"cost":0.059808,"